# SUNU GO-LIVE READINESS (SUNU-GO-LIVE-READINESS-1)

Companion to `164-front-office-branch-context-lock.mdc`, which owns the lock
MECHANICS. This rule owns the go-live POSTURE and the instrument that measures
it. Where the two touch, 164 wins on mechanics.

## The first patient must be real

FULL-PATIENT-ESTATE-RESET-1 removed the entire fictional pre-go-live estate.
Measured read-only on the pilot 2026-09-24: `mst_patients` 0, visits 0, medical
records 0, odontograms 0, consents 0, prescriptions 0, RME invoices 0, RME
payments 0, lab orders 0, lab case candidates 0, legacy RME records 0, legacy
patient staging 0, patient documents 0, SATUSEHAT candidates 0, patient-workflow
notifications 0. Deliberately PRESERVED: `sys_audit_logs` and the 43
`trx_satusehat_audit_logs` rows.

1. **A zero patient estate is the expected state, not a fault.** Do not "repair"
   it, re-seed it, or treat an empty list as a broken page.
2. **The first patient registered after the reset must be a REAL clinical
   patient.** Never manufacture one — not to demonstrate a workflow, not to
   produce go-live evidence, not to make a report look complete.
3. **Readiness is proven by tests, fixtures and read-only production
   inspection.** A production fake patient, visit, RME, odontogram, invoice or
   payment is never acceptable evidence, and its absence is never a gap in the
   evidence.
4. **If a patient exists when you expected zero, STOP and report it.** It may be
   the first real one. It is never "leftover fictional data" to clean up.

## Armed state is measured, never inferred

The cohort lives in an environment string, so the armed set is invisible to the
repository by design. That is a deliberate safety property and also a reporting
hazard: a typo looks exactly like a working lock.

5. **`php artisan rbac:front-office-branch-pin-audit` is the instrument.** It is
   the only supported way to establish who is armed on a running host. Do not
   answer the question by reading the environment string and reasoning about it,
   and never by opening a REPL on production.
6. **The audit PERSISTS NOTHING and has no arming flag.** Arming stays a
   supervised ceremony (rule 164). A command that could arm a front desk from a
   shell would make that ceremony optional.
6a. **"Read-only" is a property to be PROVEN, not assumed from the absence of a
   `save()`.** Resolving the branch authorities reaches
   `UserOnlineContextService::currentContextFor()`, which lazily calls
   `markExpiredInactive()` on an expired session — a write, reached through code
   that contains no write verb of its own. The first version of this auditor
   shipped with a green "writes nothing" test and still expired a real
   front-desk session row on production, because every fixture context was
   FRESH and never took the expiry branch. Any future read-only tool that
   touches these resolvers must resolve inside a rolled-back transaction, and
   its test must include an AGED row, not only a fresh one.
7. **It reports three independently resolved branches per account** — the pin,
   `BranchContext::forUser()` and `resolveActiveBranchForAdmin()` (the branch a
   NEW VISIT registers at) — and compares them. They have disagreed before; a
   test proves the split brain is closed in the code, this proves it is closed on
   the host that is running.
8. **`--strict` exits 2 on any anomaly** and is safe to depend on as a go-live or
   deploy gate.

## Cabang Sunu's armed posture

9. **Sunu is `SPN4`** (branch id 5 on the pilot), active and RME-enabled, with
   two ACTIVE rooms `SPN-A` / `SPN-B`. `SUN4` is a deprecated alias — see
   `136-sunu-branch-code-canonical.mdc`.
10. **Admin Sunu is armed as a COHORT OF ONE.** Admin Landak, Admin Antang and
    Admin Telkomas are deliberately NOT armed by this workstream; their
    activation is a separate ceremony each. Production carries eight Front Office
    accounts and only the approved four may ever enter the cohort.
11. **`front_office.branch_device_lock` stays OFF.** Sunu's front desk holds no
    registered WebAuthn credential, and arming the device flag would deny that
    desk its login. Branch context was armed precisely because it demands no
    proof — do not "complete" the lock by turning the device flag on.
12. **Arming Admin Sunu was safe because the pin AGREED with the live context.**
    The account's online context and four consecutive daily branch contexts all
    read SPN4 before arming, so the pin narrowed nothing in practice. Check that
    agreement before arming any further account; a pin that contradicts a live
    daily context is a fail-closed lockout, not a narrowing.

## Audit the canonical checkout, not whichever one is open

13. **Resolve and read the CANONICAL branch before reporting a defect.** During
    this workstream two "defects" — a stale branch-code registry in the rules and
    a phantom-branch hazard in `RmeBranchSeeder` — were both artifacts of
    grepping the primary checkout while it sat on an unrelated older CI-evidence
    branch. Both were already fixed on canonical. A finding measured against the
    wrong tree is not a finding, and reporting one costs more than finding
    nothing.
14. **Production HEAD, canonical HEAD and the working checkout are three
    different refs.** State which one every claim was measured against.
